I can't stop listening to the Protomen  I've known about this band and their  Megaman inspired tunes for years now but  after experiencing light up the night  through the immediacy of rock band I  suddenly understand how awesome they  truly are that in essence is the power  of rock band it harnesses the intrinsic  joy of music and presents it in a way  that not only can we play but share as  well  it's a rare accomplishment one that  propels the franchise to great heights  in the late 2000s and that beats again  in the chest of rock band 4  while this fourth installment ditches  rockband threes button having pro guitar  and superfluous keyboard controller the  core gameplay remains largely unchanged  you can grab a classic 5 button guitar  controller plastic drum kit or USB  microphone to play along with a variety  of licensed tracks matching scrolling  color-coded notes in time with the music  so yes it's exactly the gameplay you  already know and love great as that  gameplay may be though rock band 4 does  little to distinguish itself from  earlier games outside of a handful of  hit-or-miss ideas in some cases it even  takes a small step backwards take for  example the game's loosely story driven  campaign mode remember those loading  screens from rock band 2 that showed  your band casually hanging around a  practice space or riding the subway with  all their instruments simple as they  were those scenes told a story this game  nothing but menu text which you know  kind of ruins any feeling of immersion  or investment the game also forces you  to use pre created stand-in characters  any time your friends aren't present to  play as your custom characters which  means it's actually impossible to  maintain a consistent lineup which is a  pretty frustrating betrayal of the rock  and roll fantasy the campaign is trying  to fulfill outside of the campaign rock  band 4 offers a new slightly richer  quick play mode appropriately named play  a show when the track ends play a show  presents players with a selection of  songs and lets band members vote on what  to play next it's novel idea but kind of  pointless when you're standing in the  same room as the rest of your band rock  band 4 is one truly original and truly  exceptional idea is freestyle solos with  this option selected solo sections trade  notes for subtler visual cues there are  basically suggestions for how to use the  solo techniques regardless of whether  you follow along or just totally do your  own thing though the game will produce a  unique new solo that matches your inputs  mashing buttons at random creates a  disruptive cacophony but if you  implement its deeper techniques in a  thoughtful way you can actually create  some really cool sounding stuff like  surprisingly cool it's not the same  adrenaline-fueled fist-bumping thrill of  nailing every note in a really technical  section but there is an unexpected sense  of discovery and reward  similarly drummers now have dynamic  fills which pulled a random pre authored  fill from a library providing a welcome  compromise between the chaos of classic  fills and the predictability of static  fills of course all of this is nothing  without the right soundtrack and sadly  rock band 4 contains the weakest  selection of songs of any rock band game  to date that doesn't mean the whole  games The Lost Cause though there are  some seriously inspired choices here  Protomen being a prime example I was  consistently pleasantly surprised by the  tracks I didn't note beforehand and when  it comes to the classics I have just one  word for you  Panama  plus you'll be able to redownload any  dlc you already own at least in theory  and you can always buy new tracks it's  disappointing rock band 4 song offerings  aren't stronger but at least the rock  band platform remains as strong as ever  rock band 4 recaptures the unadulterated  joy that made the series such a hit but  mainly because it's a relatively  unchanged repackage rock band to a lack  of content and general stagnation hold  this particular edition of rock band  back but new ideas like the freestyle  solos genuinely enhanced the core  experience which remains a sincere and  joyful celebration of music  Oh  you
 

